{red|**Think about your spouse's experience of intimacy.** Not what you wish it was, but what it actually seems to be based on what you have observed over the years.

What do you think they need to feel truly intimate with you?}{red| What helps them feel connected?}{red| What might they be experiencing that you have misread or dismissed in the past?}

Write what you are coming to understand about your spouse's experience of intimacy:

{red|**Honestly assess the emotional safety in your marriage, specifically how safe you have made it for your spouse to be vulnerable with you.**

Does your spouse seem comfortable being vulnerable with you, or do they keep their guard up?}{red| Have you done things that made it unsafe for them to be open?}{red| Do they know they can tell you hard things without you exploding or shutting down?}{red| Have you ever used their vulnerability against them?}

{red|What has your spouse experienced with you that might have eroded their sense of safety?} Write your honest assessment:

{red|**Now consider the other direction.** What hurts from your spouse are you still carrying?}{red| What happened that you have never fully healed from?}

{red|**This is not about building a case against them or keeping score.** It is about naming what is true so it can actually be addressed and you can move forward.

What wounds are you carrying that affect your ability to be intimate with your spouse?}

{red|What is your growing edge with emotional intimacy?}{red| Where do you need to stretch?}

**Maybe you need to ask better questions instead of settling for surface conversation.** Maybe you need to listen better when your spouse shares instead of jumping to fix or defend. Maybe you need to be more vulnerable yourself instead of waiting for them to go first. Maybe you need to create more time for deep conversation instead of letting busyness crowd it out.

Identify one thing you will do differently to deepen emotional intimacy in your marriage:

{red|What practical factors are working against physical intimacy in your marriage?}

**Be specific about time constraints you face, energy levels at the times you are typically together, privacy issues in your home, problems with your physical environment, and scheduling mismatches between you and your spouse.**

Name your specific barriers:

{red|**Reflect honestly on your patterns around initiation.**

If you are usually the initiator, how do you handle rejection?}{red| Have you created pressure instead of invitation?}{red| Does your response to not tonight punish your spouse?}

{red|If you are usually the responder or decliner, how do you decline?}{red| Is it kind?}{red| Do you ever initiate?}{red| Does your spouse actually know they are desired by you?}

{red|What patterns do you recognize in yourself that might be contributing to problems?}

{red|**Through this journey, you have explored how the intimacy gap forms and how to close it.** You have identified the specific blockers in your marriage. You have learned how emotional and physical intimacy connect and feed each other. You have seen how men and women often experience intimacy differently.

**You have also worked on building emotional safety for vulnerability, healing hurts that block closeness, understanding the power of non-sexual touch, developing practices for emotional depth, creating an environment for physical intimacy, and learning healthy patterns of initiating and responding.**

**This is significant work.** The question now is what will you continue doing?}
